Deduplicate the matrix structure as it is built, instead of accumulating it.

mastruct.c builds the sparse structure by pushing ONE linked-list entry per
(dof,dof) pair of every element onto mast1/next -- growing them 1.1x through a
32-bit ITG index -- and compressing only afterwards, in the sort + "removing
duplicate entries" passes.  So the transient list is the SUM of the
per-element patterns while the structure that survives is their UNION.

For F-barES-FEM-T4 those differ by ~24x.  Measured on the LCOL4 sea ice cell,
undrained, c = 1:

  L_mesh    pairs offered   distinct (final nnz)   ratio
  0.0240      4.02e8              16 378 633       24.6x
  0.0120      1.66e9              68 318 770       24.3x
  0.0060      9.47e9             ~4.06e8           23.3x

The same (dof,dof) pair is produced by ~24 different edges, because a U3
element's ring x support stencil overlaps its neighbours' heavily.  The
duplication is entirely ACROSS elements: within one element the jj/ll loop
already emits each pair exactly once, so a per-element "direct assembly" of
U3's known ring x support pattern cannot remove any of it.  (Counted on the
0.0120 deck: the loop makes 1.4394e9 U3 insertions, while sum(3*nring * 3*nope)
-- the size a direct builder would preallocate -- is 1.5626e9, LARGER.)

What the transient costs is what blocks the finer meshes.  At 0.0120 it is
11.7 GB and the run peaks at 22.3 GB; at 0.0080 it is 4.5e9 entries and at
0.0060 9.5e9, both past the 2^31 = 2.147e9 an ITG can index, so ccx dies in
u_realloc with a negative allocation size.  INTSIZE64 raises that ceiling but
doubles the entry to 16 B, needing ~70 GB and ~150 GB -- neither machine has
it.  Removing the transient is the only route to those two meshes.

  mastructdedup.c   the deduplicating sink
  mastruct.c        routes insert()/insertfreq() through it and, when it is
                    armed, builds irow/jq straight from the sorted key set --
                    and does not allocate the linked list at all
  CalculiX.h        prototypes
  Makefile.inc      new source

HOW IT WORKS.  Pairs are packed into 64-bit keys (column in the high half,
row in the low half, so sorting the key sorts by column then row -- exactly
the order jq/irow need) and buffered.  When the buffer fills it is
radix-sorted, uniqued, and back-merged into one running sorted run; the merge
writes downward into the grown run array, so it needs no scratch.  Peak memory
is the buffer plus the final structure, independent of how much duplication
there is.  The buffer starts at 4 MB and doubles up to CCX_MASTRUCT_BUF
megabytes (default 512), so a small model never allocates more than it needs.

ONE BACKEND, DELIBERATELY.  An earlier revision also carried an
open-addressing hash-set sink.  It produced the identical structure but was
measured slower for it -- 58.4 s against 26.2 s on the 0.0120 structure,
paying for random probes into a table too big to cache -- and its footprint
scaled with the structure instead of being a number you set.  It has been
removed rather than left as a second way to do the same thing.

The element loop and every SPC/MPC branch are untouched -- only the sink the
pairs flow into changes -- so the constrained-DOF expansion the generic path
performs is performed identically here.  Substructures (mastructread) and the
frequency/modal boundary block still walk ipointer/next directly, so neither
arms the backend: nmethod 2/4/5-7 stays on the legacy path, and a superelement
is a hard error telling you to rerun with CCX_MASTRUCT_DEDUP=legacy.  That
variable is the escape hatch for bisecting a suspected regression; there is no
other setting.

VERIFIED ANSWER-NEUTRAL.  Identical structure and identical reactions against
the legacy path, on the acceptance decks (LCOL4, undrained, c = 1, unsymmetric
path, PARDISO out of core):

  L_mesh   path      equations   nonzero lower triangular   F_x on RP-1
  0.0240   legacy      177 848            16 378 633        1.552992E+07
  0.0240   dedup       177 848            16 378 633        1.552992E+07
  0.0120   legacy      603 467            68 318 770        1.208091E+07
  0.0120   dedup       603 467            68 318 770        1.208091E+07

i.e. E_x = 6.212 GPa at 0.0240 and 4.832 GPa at 0.0120, the values the legacy
binary returns.  The remaining differences in the .dat files are the transverse
reactions on RP-2/RP-3, ~1e-14 of the driving reaction, which differ by the
same amount between two runs of the SAME binary -- the threaded assembly is not
bitwise reproducible, and that predates this patch.

WHAT IT BUYS.  At 0.0120 the structure phase drops from an 11.7 GB transient to
3.5 GB peak and 26 s.  The total peak RSS does NOT improve there, because the
out-of-core factorisation's core budget dominates once the structure is built.
The point is not 0.0120 -- it is that at 0.0080 and 0.0060 the legacy path
cannot build the structure AT ALL, at any amount of RAM, and with this it
builds: 187 006 594 nonzeros at 0.0080, 4.06e8 at 0.0060.
